WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
So very quickly, yesterday we looked at the request and response cycle for an API.

00:08.000 --> 00:15.000
Think of routing in Flask as the same thing. Flask is our web application.

00:15.000 --> 00:26.000
Consider our server. So our client is the browser. In this case, I'm asking for the root URL for my server.

00:27.000 --> 00:33.000
And my server is returning a response, just a plain old string that says hello world.

00:39.000 --> 00:49.000
Declaring routes in Flask, we saw the, that forward slash resolves to the root.

00:49.000 --> 00:58.000
But we can also put in as many paths as we want. Let's say,

00:58.000 --> 01:14.000
funjora worlds returns, funjora worlds. By default, we want all of our visitors to see hello worlds.

01:14.000 --> 01:21.000
But optionally, if they want to take the special path for, let's say, France.

01:21.000 --> 01:29.000
We can do that. So we would want to declare a new app route. So still the forward slash, that's the root.

01:29.000 --> 01:35.000
But now I'm going to say forward slash French.

01:35.000 --> 01:42.000
And make sure that your, your Flask app is running here.

01:42.000 --> 01:47.000
Before we do that, I'm going to show you something pretty handy.

01:47.000 --> 02:00.000
In Flask, you can have a debug mode for your local environment where you can have your site, just live reload your code.

02:00.000 --> 02:06.000
So that you don't have to type your new function, change your route, play around with things.

02:06.000 --> 02:15.000
Kill the server, start it back up again, pretty tedious process. So here, just like we set the Flask app to be hello.py.

02:15.000 --> 02:21.000
We want to back up a little bit. And we want to set a new environment variable.

02:21.000 --> 02:29.000
And we're going to set Flask underscore n to equal development.

02:29.000 --> 02:32.000
So I'm just going to copy that.

02:32.000 --> 02:39.000
So for Mac, it would be this. And for Windows.

